      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we can use the trigonometric identity for the cosine of a sum of angles. Specifically, we can use the identity \\(\\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B = \\cos(A + B)\\). In this case, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos(2x + 3x) = \\cos 5x.\n\\]\nThus, the given equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = 0.\n\\]\nThe solutions to \\(\\cos 5x = 0\\) are given by:\n\\[\n5x = 90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k,\n\\]\nfor any integer \\(k\\). To find the smallest positive value of \\(x\\), we set \\(k = 0\\):\n\\[\n5x = 90^\\circ \\implies x = \\frac{90^\\circ}{5} = 18^\\circ.\n\\]\nTherefore, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}.\n\\]",
