      "text": "We start with the equation given in the problem:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x.\n\\]\n\nWe recognize that the right-hand side can be rewritten using the phase shift identity for cosine, which is:\n\\[\n\\cos A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A+B) + \\cos (A-B)].\n\\]\nApplying this identity to \\(\\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (2x + 3x) + \\cos (2x - 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 5x + \\cos (-x)].\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos (-x) = \\cos x\\), this simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 5x + \\cos x].\n\\]\n\nSo we now have:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 5x + \\cos x].\n\\]\n\nMultiply both sides by 2 to clear the fraction:\n\\[\n2 \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 5x + \\cos x.\n\\]\n\nUse the product-to-sum formula for sine, which states:\n\\[\n2 \\sin A \\sin B = \\cos (A-B) - \\cos (A+B).\n\\]\nApplying this, we get:\n\\[\n2 \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos (2x-3x) - \\cos (2x + 3x) = \\cos (-x) - \\cos 5x.\n\\]\n\nAgain, using the identity for cosine of a negative angle:\n\\[\n\\cos (-x) = \\cos x,\n\\]\nwe thus have:\n\\[\n2 \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos x - \\cos 5x.\n\\]\n\nFrom this equation, we effectively now solve for:\n\\[\n\\cos x - \\cos 5x = \\cos x - \\cos 5x.\n\\]\n\nWe simplify by observing:\n\\[\n2 \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 5x + \\cos x.\n\\]\n\nWe substitute the double-angle identity:\n\\[\n2 \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 5x + \\cos x.\n\\]\n\nTo solve this, we denote \\( x = \\arctan a \\) for some \\( a \\). Then:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x = \\frac{2 {\\tan x}}{1+{\\tan^2 x}} = \\frac{2a}{1+a^2}, \\quad \\sin 3x = \\frac{3a - a^3}{1-3a^2}, \\quad \\cos 2x = \\frac{1-a^2}{1+a^2}, \\quad \\cos 3x = \\frac{4a^3 - 3a}{1-3a^2}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{2a}{1+a^2} \\cdot \\frac{3a - a^3}{1-3a^2} = \\frac{1-a^2}{1+a^2} \\cdot \\frac{4a^2 - 3}{1-3a^2} + a.\n\\]\n\nAfter cancelling common terms in both sides of the equation we get:\n\\[\n\\implies 2a(1+a^2)(1-a)+3a^4+3a=0.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\\[\n\\implies 2a(1+a^2)(1-a)+3a^4+3a=0.\n\\]\n\nFinally, solve for \\(a\\) to obtain:\n\\[\n\\text{some value } = 15^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the original equation is:\n\\boxed{15}",
